## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code aborts a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with task-based execution.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | The sync overload can't force `work()` to stop—it just stops *waiting*. The delegate keeps running in the background until it finishes. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaks timers otherwise. |
| **Not passing token into work** | If `work` does I/O or loops, it must accept and check a `CancellationToken` for true cooperative cancellation. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish your timeout from external cancellation. |

### Critical Behavioral Difference

With `Thread.Abort`, the work was **forcibly terminated**. With cooperative cancellation, **the work only stops if it checks the token**. If `work()` is a tight CPU loop or a blocking call that ignores the token, it continues running after the timeout—you just stop waiting for it.

For truly uncooperative code, your only option is `Process.Kill` on a child process.

### Async usage example

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// Work cooperates with cancellation
var result = await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async ct =>
{
    await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, ct);  // respects token
    return ProcessData();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Removed APIs summary

| Old | New |
|-----|-----|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Remove entirely |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(work, token)` |
